@TITLE@ QilinRecomp for Linux (64-bit)
=============================================

Raspberry Pi: this needs the 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS. On the 32-bit one,
"Play @TITLE@" can't start (the system says it can't execute it, or that
the file isn't found). In a terminal, "getconf LONG_BIT" says 64 on a
64-bit system.

@FULL_TITLE@ (@STUDIO@; Rhythm Heaven Fever in North America),
recompiled to run natively: the game's own program translated into C++ and
compiled for your computer, drawn with Vulkan.

No game data is included. You need your own copy of the game: @RELEASE@,
extracted into a folder with Dolphin. Other regions and revisions (Rhythm
Heaven Fever, Minna no Rhythm Tengoku) are other programs and aren't
supported.


Your game folder
----------------
In Dolphin, right-click the game in the list, choose Properties, open the
Filesystem tab, right-click the disc at the top and choose Extract Entire
Disc, and pick an empty folder. The folder you need is the one holding
"sys" and "files": if Dolphin made a DATA folder inside the one you picked,
it is that DATA folder. (Wiimms ISO Tools' "wit extract" makes the same
folder.) A .wbfs, .iso or .rvz file can't be read as it is: extract it
first.


Playing
-------
1. Unpack the download anywhere you can write to (your home folder is
   fine): double-click it in your file manager, or in a terminal
   "tar xzf @PACKAGE@-*.tar.gz". Building the game needs about 2 GB free.
2. Open the @PACKAGE@ folder and double-click "Play @TITLE@" (if your file
   manager asks, choose Execute or Run). From a terminal: ./"Play @TITLE@"
3. Click "Choose your extracted disc" and pick the folder above, or drag it
   onto the window. (Where the desktop has no folder chooser, type or paste
   the folder's path.) It tells you straight away if it is the wrong game,
   another region or another revision, or if part of the disc is missing.
4. Wait for the first build: a few minutes on a desktop PC. Your game
   files never leave your computer.
5. Click Play. The window hides while the game runs. "Add to the
   applications menu" puts @TITLE@ in your desktop's menu.

After that, Play @TITLE@ remembers your folder (even in a new download
folder), and Play starts the game straight away. After an update it builds
the game again first, recompiling only what changed. Leave the disc folder
where it is: the game reads its files from it while you play.

The game's output is in work/run.log and work/run-errors.log, and the
build's in work/build.log. Send them when reporting a problem. If the
picture stays black after the first screen, close the game and start it
again (a rare fault at start-up, not yet found).

Needs: a 64-bit Linux on x86-64 (the linux-x86_64 download) or ARM64 (the
linux-arm64 download) with glibc 2.35 or later (Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12,
Fedora 36, Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm or later), and a graphics driver with
Vulkan 1.3: Mesa's Vulkan drivers (on Debian and Ubuntu, the
mesa-vulkan-drivers package) or NVIDIA's. Sound goes through PipeWire,
PulseAudio or ALSA, whichever the system has. Before building, Play
@TITLE@ checks for Vulkan and says what to install if it's missing.
Updates use curl (or wget) and tar, which nearly every Linux has.


Raspberry Pi
------------
- The 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS (see the top of this file), on a Raspberry Pi
  4 or 5 with 2 GB of memory or more. Use the linux-arm64 download.
- Vulkan 1.3 for the Pi's graphics arrived with Mesa 24.3: Raspberry Pi OS
  Trixie (October 2025) or later has it. On an older Raspberry Pi OS, Play
  @TITLE@ says so before building: update Raspberry Pi OS.
- The first build compiles the whole game on the Pi itself, so it takes
  much longer than on a desktop PC, and longer still on a 2 GB Pi, which
  compiles fewer files at a time. Leave it running. Later builds take
  compiled files from the cache.
- In the settings menu, use the "low-end" or "original" preset (both draw
  the console's own 640x528 picture), keep vsync on, and play full screen
  (F11).
- "Add to the applications menu" (once the game is built) puts @TITLE@ in
  the Raspberry Pi menu, under Games.


Controls
--------
A Wii Remote held upright, buttons only (no motion controls).

  Controller               Keyboard                     Wii Remote
  south button (Xbox A),   A, Right Shift, Right        A
  right shoulder           arrow, Space
  east button (Xbox B),    B, Left Shift, Left arrow    B (the trigger)
  left shoulder, right
  trigger
  west, north buttons      1, 2                         1, 2
  Start, Back              Enter; Backspace or Tab      +; -
  Guide                    H                            HOME
  d-pad, left stick        I J K L, Up and Down arrows  the d-pad

On the title screen, press A and B together. Every connected controller
plays, and rumble reaches them.

F1 or Esc (a controller: Back and Start together): the settings menu (the
game pauses), with the controls (set by pressing them), the picture (size,
a CRT look, internal resolution, filtering, vsync) and the console's own
settings. Alt+Enter or F11: full screen. F3: frame times, the display's
refresh and the sound's lag.

Settings (beatthebeat.ini), saves (nand) and the shader cache are kept in
~/.local/share/qilin/@GAME@ (or $XDG_DATA_HOME/qilin/@GAME@), with the
folder you chose and the build's cache of compiled files (build-cache).


What's in here
--------------
Play @TITLE@      chooses your extracted disc, builds the game (once) and starts it
kit/qilin           the recompiler: turns the disc's @BOOT@ into C++
kit/rhf             the Rhythm Heaven Fever decompilation's function names,
                    which the recompiler matches to your disc's code
kit/lib, include    the prebuilt rest of the game: its runtime, the renderer,
                    sound, the window and controls (SDL3), the menu (Dear ImGui)
kit/toolchain/zig   zig, a C++ compiler (Clang, with its own libc++)
kit/icon.png        the menu entry's icon
kit/files.txt       every file this release holds (see below)
work/               made when building: the game (@EXE_LINUX@), built from your
                    disc, the recompiled code, the build's files and the logs
LICENSES/           the licences of everything included

zig keeps its own cache of compiled libraries in ~/.cache/zig.

Source, changes and issues: https://github.com/@REPO@


Updates and recovery
--------------------
"Check for updates" in Play @TITLE@ asks GitHub for a newer release (it
sends nothing about you or your computer). If there is one, it downloads
it, unpacks it beside this folder and swaps in the new kit and launcher,
then starts again and builds the game for the new version. If anything
fails on the way, the old version is left as it was. You can also unpack a
new download anywhere, even over this folder: it finds your folder and the
cache by itself, and at its first start it removes files the new kit no
longer has (anything in kit/ or LICENSES/ that kit/files.txt doesn't name).
Your saves and settings are never in this folder, so nothing of yours is
touched.

The game is compiled for this computer's processor; moved to another
computer, it is built again. Compiled files come from the cache when
nothing they're made from has changed. A failed build leaves the working
game as it was; the one before a new build is kept as
work/@EXE_LINUX@.previous until the new one has run and closed normally.
"Build again from scratch" compiles everything anew.
